"ENC_MIMG":["Memory image (texture) operations.",64,[["A16","Texture coordinates size: 1 = 16 bit, 0 = 32 bit.",16,1],["D16","If set, convert 32 bit floating point texture return data to 16 bit.",17,1],["DIM","Dimensionality of resource constant, used to determine the number of addresses supplied for this instruction.",2,3],["DLC","If set, operation is L1-coherent (synced between WGPs in a shader engine).",13,1],["DMASK","Enable mask for image read/write data components. Normally bit 0 is red, 1 is green, 2 is blue and 3 is alpha. At least 1 bit must be on. Data is assumed to be packed into consecutive VGPRs.\n\nFor reads, defines which components are returned.\n\nFor writes, defines which components are written with data from VGPRs (missing components get 0).\n\nEnabled components come from consecutive VGPRs. For example when DMASK = 'b1001 the red component is in VDATA0 and the alpha component is in VDATA1.\n\nFor D16 reads, DMASK indicates which components to return.\n\nFor D16 writes, the DMASK indicates which components to write but has restrictions: Data is read out of consecutive VGPRs: LSBs of VDATA, then MSBs of VDATA, then LSBs of VDATA+1 and last if needed MSBs of VDATA+1. This is regardless of which DMASK bits are set, only how many bits are set. The position of the DMASK bits controls which components are written in memory.\n\nIf DMASK==0, the TA overrides with DMASK = 1 and puts zeros in VGPR followed by LWE status if exists. TFE status is not generated since the fetch is dropped.\n\nFor IMAGE_GATHER4* instructions, DMASK indicates which component (RGBA), and the number of VGPRs to use is determined automatically by hardware (4 VGPRs when D16=0, and 2 VGPRs when D16=1).",8,4],["ENCODING","Encoding.",26,6],["GLC","If set, operation is globally coherent. Ignored for STORE operations.",14,1],["LWE","LOD Warning Enable (for partially resident textures).",54,1],["NSA","Non Sequential Address mode: triggers reading one additional DWORD in the instruction stream to obtain up to 4 additional address components. See MIMG_2 token.",0,1],["OP","Opcode.",18,8],["R128","Texture resource size: 1 = 128 bit (4 SGPRs), 0 = 256 bit (8 SGPRs).",15,1],["SLC","System Level Coherent.",12,1],["SRSRC","Scalar GPR that specifies the resource constant, in units of 4 SGPRs.",48,5],["SSAMP","Scalar GPR that specifies the sampler constant, in units of 4 SGPRs.",58,5],["TFE","Texture Fail Enable (for partially resident textures).",53,1],["UNORM","Force address to be un-normalized regardless of texture sampler setting. Must be set to 1 for image store and atomic operations.\n\n0: for image ops with samplers, (S,T,R) coordinates in [0.0, 1.0] span the entire texture map.\n\n1: for image ops with samplers, (S,T,R) coordinates in [0.0, N] span the texture map where N is (width,height,depth).\n\nArray index, cube slice, LOD, bias etc. are not affected. Image ops without a sampler are not affected. UINT inputs are unnormalized. This bit is logically OR'd with the SAMP.FORCE_UNNORMALIZED when sent to the texture unit.",7,1],["VADDR","First VGPR component to provide address information.",32,8],["VDATA","Vector GPR to write result to.",40,8]]],
"ENC_EXP":["Graphics export operations.",64,[["DONE","If set, this is the last export of a given type. If this is set for a colour export (PS only), then the valid mask must be present in the EXEC register.",11,1],["EN","Indicate which VSRC selects should be exported to SX.\n\n16-bit components: export half-dword enable. Valid values are 0, 1, 3. Bit 0 enables VSRC0: R,G from one VGPR (R in low bits, G high), bit 1 enables VSRC1: B,A from one VGPR (B in low bits, A high).\n\n32-bit components: bit N enables VSRC[N].\n\nEN must not be zero.",0,4],["ENCODING","Encoding.",26,6],["ROW_EN","If set, use M0 to provide the row number for mesh shader's POS and PRIM exports.",13,1],["TGT","Export target.",4,6],["VSRC0","VGPR of the first data to export.",32,8],["VSRC1","VGPR of the second data to export.",40,8],["VSRC2","VGPR of the third data to export.",48,8],["VSRC3","VGPR of the fourth data to export.",56,8]]],
