"VOP1_VOP_DPP16":["Vector ALU operations with one destination and one source. Instructions in this encoding may be promoted to VOP3 unless otherwise noted. Instructions in this encoding may use DPP modes unless otherwise noted.",64,[["BANK_MASK","Bank enable mask. If bit N is set, then lanes[4*N:4*N+3, 4*N+16:4*N+19, 4*N+32:4*N+35, 4*N+48:4*N+51] are enabled.",56,4],["BOUND_CTRL","Specifies behaviour when shared data is invalid. 0: set write-enable on the affected lane to zero. 1: set source data for the affected lane to zero.",51,1],["DPP_CTRL","Data-parallel primitive control.",40,9],["ENCODING","Encoding.",25,7],["FI","If true, reads GPRs when fetching data from an inactive lane (Fetch Inactive mode). If false, reads ZERO when fetching data from an inactive lane.",50,1],["OP","Opcode.",9,8],["ROW_MASK","Row enable mask. If bit N is set, then lanes[16*N:16*N+15] are enabled.",60,4],["SRC0","First source operand for instruction.",0,9],["SRC0_ABS","If true, take the floating-point absolute value of src0. Note: this field is ignored when DPP is used in VOP3/VOP3P encodings; use the fields provided by those encodings instead.",53,1],["SRC0_NEG","If true, take the floating-point negation of src0. Note: this field is ignored when DPP is used in VOP3/VOP3P encodings; use the fields provided by those encodings instead.",52,1],["SRC1_ABS","If true, take the floating-point absolute value of src1. Note: this field is ignored when DPP is used in VOP3/VOP3P encodings; use the fields provided by those encodings instead.",55,1],["SRC1_NEG","If true, take the floating-point negation of src1. Note: this field is ignored when DPP is used in VOP3/VOP3P encodings; use the fields provided by those encodings instead.",54,1],["VDST","Destination operand for instruction.",17,8],["VSRC0","VGPR address for first source operand.",32,8]]],
"VOP3_INST_LITERAL":["Vector ALU operations requiring three sources or modifiers; also some obscure opcodes that cannot be encoded in smaller instructions due to limited OP bits. Almost all VOP1, VOP2 and VOPC opcodes can be promoted to VOP3 to use input and output modifiers.",96,[["ABS","If ABS[N] set, take the floating-point absolute value of the N'th input operand. This is applied before negation. See OPF_NOABS for instructions that do not allow this input modifier.",8,3],["CLAMP","If set, clamp or signal on output. VCMP instructions enable signaling-compare. For floating point outputs, clamp to [0.0, 1.0]. For signed integer outputs, clamp to [MIN_INT, MAX_INT]. For unsigned integer outputs, clamp to [0, MAX_UINT]. Applied after output modifier. See OPF_NOCLAMP for a list of instructions that do not support clamping or signaling.",15,1],["ENCODING","Encoding.",26,6],["LITERAL","32-bit literal constant.",64,32],["NEG","If NEG[N] set, take the floating-point negation of the N'th input operand. This is applied after absolute value. See OPF_NONEG for a list of instructions that do not support negation.",61,3],["OMOD","Output modifier for instruction. Only useful for floating point outputs; integer outputs should set this to OMOD_OFF. Applied before clamping. See OPF_NOOMOD for a list of instructions that do not support output modifier.\n\nOutput modifiers apply to single and double floating point results only. Integer and packed float 16 results ignore the omod setting.\n\nOutput modifiers are not compatible with output denorms. If output denorms are allowed, then any output modifier is ignored. If output denorms are not allowed, then denorms are flushed to +/-0 and the output modifier is applied. If modifier is non-zero, -0 is forced to +0. If an instruction forces output denorm to be disabled then the output modifier can be applied on the result no matter the output denorm setting.\n\nOutput modifiers are not IEEE compliant (-0*x=+0). Output modifiers are ignored by hardware if ieee mode is set.",59,2],["OP","Opcode.",16,10],["OP_SEL","For 16-bit inputs, OP_SEL[N] determines if lower [15:0] or upper [31:16] bits are selected for the N'th source operand. Similarly OP_SEL[3] controls whether the lower [15:0] or upper [31:16] bits are written for a 16-bit destination (preserving the unwritten bits). 0 selects the lower order bits and 1 selects the higher order bits.",11,4],["SRC0","First source operand for instruction.",32,9],["SRC1","Second source operand for instruction.",41,9],["SRC2","Third source operand for instruction.",50,9],["VDST","Destination operand for instruction.",0,8]]],
